<Base
  title="Become a Resident in Andorra | Official pathways"
  description="Andorran residency by official letter code (A.1, J.1, D.1) mapped to a practitioner A/B/C/D guide. Law 2/2026 figures from BOPA. Not legal advice."
  path="/become-a-resident"
>
  <section class="page-header">
    <h1>Become a Resident</h1>
    <p>
      Andorra is a sovereign principality. Residence is granted under Andorran
      immigration law, through the Servei d’Immigració. This page is an
      orientation, not advice.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        Official sources win. The Government’s letter codes — A.1, J.1, D.1,
        B.1 — are not the same letters that some English advisors use for
        “passive A / company B / fame C / Compte Propi D”. Read the live
        procedure on
        <a
          href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia-i-treball"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Govern.ad</a
        >
        and the 2026 text of
        <a
          href="https://bopadocuments.blob.core.windows.net/bopa-documents/038015/pdf/CGL_2026_02_12_10_11_12.pdf"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Law 2/2026 in the BOPA</a
        >
        before you act.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Two alphabets, one file</h2>
    <p>
      Jane Whittaker of J W Serveis publishes an English
      <a
        href="https://www.livinginandorra.com/residency_options.html"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">2026 residency-options page</a
      >
      on livinginandorra.com. She is listed in the
      <a href="/directory/jane-whittaker">directory</a>.
      That page is a practitioner map: useful for how files are discussed in
      English rooms, not a substitute for the law. We do not copy her prose.
      Where a euro figure appears below, it is because BOPA or a live
      Govern.ad page currently states it — not because a blog does.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Her <strong>Category A</strong> (passive investment) maps onto official
        <strong>D.1</strong> residence without lucrative activity — not onto
        official A.1.
      </li>
      <li>
        Her <strong>Category B</strong> (company / business plan, 85% of
        activity outside, at most one employee) maps onto official
        <strong>D.1.2</strong> professionals with international projection. She
        discourages this route; that is her advice, not a Government closure.
      </li>
      <li>
        Her <strong>Category C</strong> (recognised standing in sport, culture
        or science) maps onto official <strong>D.1.3</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Her <strong>Category D</strong> (Compte Propi) maps onto official
        <strong>J.1</strong> self-employed residence and work.
      </li>
      <li>
        Official <strong>A.1</strong> is residence and work for an Andorran
        employer. Official <strong>B.1</strong> is family reunification.
        Official <strong>C.1</strong> is frontier work. Those letters are not
        her A, B or C.
      </li>
      <li>
        Official <strong>D.3</strong> (digital nomad) and
        <strong>D.4</strong> (entrepreneur programmes) remain on Govern.ad.
        Living in Andorra states that she does not process those files.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Residence and work</h2>
    <p>
      An authorisation to live in Andorra and work for an Andorran employer
      (A.1) generally requires a contract with a legally constituted company and
      a place within the quota then in force. Self-employed residence and work
      (J.1) is a separate initial authorisation for those who will exercise an
      activity on their own account. Both are described, with document lists, on
      the official immigration pages. Detail:
      <a href="/self-employed-residence">J.1</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first A.1 / J.1 authorisation is typically issued for one year, with
      later renewals on a longer cycle. Nationals of states that have a
      convention with Andorra may follow a different renewal path.
    </p>
    <p>
      Living in Andorra states that Compte Propi is temporarily closed pending
      a new quota. That is her 2026 operational note. It is not what the
      Government currently publishes: the J.1 page is still live; a
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/residencia-i-treball/reserva-autoritzacio-de-residencia-i-treball-per-compte-propi"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">J reservation</a
      >
      step exists; and a
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/w/aprovada-una-nova-quota-d-autoritzacions-d-immigracio-de-residencia-i-treball-per-compte-propi-i-de-treball-sense-residencia"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">Govern news note</a
      >
      describes Decret 75/2026 setting a general self-employed quota of 200
      authorisations (150 for foreign-investment cases, 50 for liberal
      professions). Confirm whether a place is actually being issued before
      you plan around either sentence.
    </p>

    <h2>Residence without work</h2>
    <p>
      Residence without work (D.1) covers people who will live in Andorra
      without taking local employment. The Government’s page currently names
      D.1.2, D.1.3 and D.1.4. A digital-nomad authorisation (D.3) and a route
      for participants in entrepreneur programmes (D.4) sit on the same
      official residence index. Longer notes:
      <a href="/residence-without-work">D.1</a>,
      <a href="/digital-nomad">D.3</a>,
      <a href="/entrepreneur-residence">D.4</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Several of those pages still say principal and effective residence for
      at least 90 days per calendar year. Tax residence under article 8 of
      Law 5/2014 is a different test — more than 183 days, or the centre of
      economic interests. That note is on
      <a href="/tax-residence">tax residence</a>.
      We do not repeat claims that immigration “does not monitor” days. The
      official texts state the conditions; they do not describe enforcement.
    </p>

    <h3>What Law 2/2026 currently states for non-lucrative residence</h3>
    <p>
      Law 2/2026 of 22 January — continuity and consolidation of measures for
      sustainable growth — was published in the BOPA on 12 February 2026 and
      entered into force on 13 February 2026. Article 96, as printed there,
      requires the principal holder of a residence without lucrative activity
      to invest, permanently and effectively, at least
      <strong>1,000,000 euros</strong> in named Andorran assets (property;
      equity in resident companies; certain debt or collective-investment
      instruments of resident issuers, for a maximum of 36 months before the
      sum must be moved to another permitted class; public-administration
      debt; life-insurance products with resident insurers; or unremunerated
      AFA deposits).
    </p>
    <p>
      If that investment is made wholly or partly in property, the same
      article requires more than <strong>800,000 euros</strong> to be applied
      to each immovable unit acquired. The million-euro total still applies:
      a purchase above 800,000 euros but below 1,000,000 euros leaves a
      balance that must sit in other permitted assets.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same article reduces the investment to
      <strong>400,000 euros</strong> where it is made, directly or
      indirectly, permanently and effectively, in the Housing Fund (Fons
      d’Habitatge), “in accordance with the applicable regulations”. Living
      in Andorra states that Immigration has not confirmed the fund is
      operating. We have not found a Govern.ad page that opens applications
      to it. Treat the 400,000-euro figure as a legal possibility, not as a
      counter you can walk up to.
    </p>
    <p>
      Article 96 also requires an income to the Andorran Financial Authority
      of <strong>50,000 euros</strong> for the principal and
      <strong>12,000 euros</strong> for each dependant who takes the same
      status. The BOPA text says those sums are definitive and not
      refundable, unless the initial immigration authorisation is refused.
      Living in Andorra states that Categories B and C may still be treated
      as refundable bonds and that Immigration does not know. That is her
      operational caveat. It is not what article 96 says for residence
      without lucrative activity. Read the article that matches your subtype
      with counsel.
    </p>

    <h3>Card-issue tariffs on Govern.ad</h3>
    <p>
      The Government’s
      <a
        href="https://www.govern.ad/ca/tematiques/immigracio/altres-tramits/tarifes"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">immigration tariffs page</a
      >
      currently lists 3,000 euros for delivery of a residence-without-work
      authorisation, 1,000 euros for a dependant resident, and 500 euros for
      renewal of residence without work. Other permit types on the same table
      carry different amounts. Confirm the line that matches your file.
    </p>

    <h2>Family, study and other statuses</h2>
    <p>
      Family reunification (B.1), study and research (F), temporary seasonal
      authorisations (G.1) and frontier work (C.1) are published on the same
      Government section. B.1 is
      <a href="/family-reunification">family reunification</a>
      — not Jane’s Category B.
    </p>

    <h2>Property purchase is a separate tax</h2>
    <p>
      A house is not a permit. Non-resident purchases need foreign-investment
      authorisation. A Government news note of 26 February 2026, giving
      effect to Law 2/2026, states that the foreign-investment property tax
      moves from 3% to 6% on acquisition of a first property, and from 5% to
      10% on a second home. Residents who cannot show three or more years of
      effective and permanent residence in the ten years before the
      application remain in that tax net (Decret 58/2026). Detail:
      <a href="/buying-property">buying as a non-resident</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What the process usually involves</h2>
    <p>
      Applications are made to the Servei d’Immigració. Official pages describe
      identity documents, legalised or apostilled criminal-record certificates,
      proof of accommodation, civil-status documents, and — for workers — a
      contract. A medical review is part of the published file. After a grant,
      the holder is expected to register with the Comú of the parish of
      residence within the period stated on the official note (three months on
      the current A.1 page).
    </p>
    <p>
      Living in Andorra states that, for Russian and Belarusian nationals,
      foreign-investment company files (her B and D) are not being processed,
      that her Category A may still proceed, and that banks are difficult.
      We have not found a matching Govern.ad circular. Treat that as her
      practice note, and confirm it with counsel and with the Register of
      Foreign Investments — not as a rule we can certify.
    </p>

